Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are attracting immense research interest while their synthesis usually proceeds in organic media or under harsh conditions dependent on specific cases. Herein, Hofmeister effect was firstly introduced into the construction of MOFs and thereafter a general salting-in species (SS) induced self-assembly strategy  was proposed for the aqueous-phase and mild synthesis of stable MOFs based on a unique “solubilization-mediating” mechanism. SS not only improved the solubility of organic ligands but also effectively mediated the mutual approaching of organic linkers and inorganic nodes, thus facilitating the crystallization of MOFs under mild conditions. Tens of typical and most used stable MOFs were exemplified thanks to the availability of various SS. This strategy could set a framework for the development of more stable MOFs in aqueous-phase and drive the large-scale and economic production of MOFs.
## Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are booming as a new class of porous and crystalline material.1,2 The advent of stable MOFs based on high-valent metal ions has aroused a new research upsurge in that their more application potentials are coming into true.3-8 Unfortunately, their traditional synthesis usually suffers from some disadvantages, including the needs of organic media, harsh conditions as well as sometimes toxic additives such as HF. In recent years, there has been an emerging interest in improving the synthesis procedure of MOFs and some significant progress has been made.9-16 Herein, we propose a universal salting-in species (SS) induced self-assembly (SSISA) strategy based on Hofmeister effect to solve these intractable issues. The key to this achievement lies in the roles SS played, which not only promote the gradual dissolution of organic ligands into water media, but also mediate the mutual proximity of organic linkers and inorganic nodes.17,18 Such a unique “solubilization-mediating” mechanism is significantly conductive to increase the reaction probability of MOFs precursors and thus facilitating the aqueous-phase construction of well-crystallized MOFs under mild conditions. We exemplified the validity of this strategy through the successful synthesis of a series of stable MOFs including UiO-66(Zr) with different topological structures,19,20 UiO-66(Hf),21 UiO-66(Ce),22 MIL-53(Al),23 MIL-101(Cr)24 as well as their derivatives. The specific self-assembly prerequisites of different stable MOFs were satisfied thanks to the availability of various SS. In most cases, the synthesis can be proceeded as simple as only the precursors of MOFs were required in the presence of SS under mild conditions. Taking UiO-66(Ce) family as an example, the reaction could be completed within 10 min even at room temperature. We expect that our strategy would present guidelines for the rational design of a broad variety of stable MOFs with remarkable properties, meanwhile, drive the largescale utilization of MOFs towards commercialization.
## Results and discussion
More than a century ago, Hofmeister series was established based on the ability of ions to precipitate proteins.25 The typical order of the anion and cation series is shown in Figure 1a.26 Generally, the ions on the left side are called salting-out ones whereas the opposite is salting-in. Hofmeister series has an important effect on aqueous solubility of solutes ranging from small molecules to biomacromolecules and polymers. For example, $^{1,4}$ -dicarboxybenzene (BDC) is the most extensively used ligand for the construction of stable MOFs, but is poorsoluble in aqueous media. The preliminary experiments display that the aqueous solubilities of BDC and its derivatives can indeed be enhanced by salting-in ions $(\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{^{-}}$ , ${\mathsf{C l O}}_{4}^{.}$ , SCN-, I- and guanidinium $\left(\mathsf{G}\mathsf{d}\mathsf{m}^{+}\right)]$ while decreased by salting-out one (Cl-) in good line with Hofmeister effect (Figure 1b, Figures S1 and S2, see ESI†). The scientific interpretation on “Hofmeister effect” has experienced continuous revision. Consensus has currently reached that a direct interaction between ions and solutes plays a critical role in the observed Hofmeister effect.27 As far as SS are concerned, they are readily to be polarized and substantial dipoles are induced so that they present a high affinity toward the hydrophobic ligands of MOFs through favorable dipoledipole interactions. The bound SS weaken the hydrophobic aggregation of organic ligands, thereby improve their aqueous solubility.28-31 It is worth noting that the underlying molecularlevel mechanisms of Hofmeister effect is quite complicated and the series of ions may be partially or even completely reversed depending on the size, polarity and charge of the solute or other properties of the solution system $32-34$ . Therefore, the solubilization ability of SS for ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ did not completely follow the typical Hofmeister ordering (Figure 1a), but the salting-in and salting-out characteristics of ions were still consistent with the traditional classification of Hofmeister effect. In addition to solubilization, abundant SS accumulated around organic ligands would simultaneously attract metal precursors, behaving as mediators to bridge the organic linkers and inorganic nodes (Figure 1c).35 The enhancement of the local concentration of MOFs precursors relative to bulk facilitates the self-assembly of MOFs in mild conditions. Therefore, the reaction can be triggered even if there are a few dissolved ligands initially. Upon prolonging reaction time, undissolved ligands are continuously pumped into the solution to compensate for the ones in the formation of MOFs until they are completely used up. Based on this, we firstly introduced the Hofmeister theory into the selfassembly of MOFs in aqueous phase, and a unique “solubilization-mediating” mechanism was summarized (Figure 1c, d).
Additionally, Hofmeister series can be extended to organic species for the formation of MOFs (Figure 1a).36 Of special attention is L-arginine thanks to its distinctive structural characteristic with both guanidine and carboxylic groups (Figure 1e). Previous research suggested that the interaction between guanidine group and the π-conjugated system endowed Larginine with remarkable solubilization capacity for aromatic compounds.37 Solubility measurements also confirm its more pronounced salting-in effect than small ions (Figures 1f and Figure S3 in ESI†). The bifunctional characteristic means that the L-arginine can serve not only as a mediator but also as a modulator with its carboxyl to bind to inorganic nodes in MOFs. Based on the above effects, different stable MOFs can be
obtained under aqueous and mild conditions simply by matching appropriate SS with specific MOFs precursors.
UiO-66(Zr) was selected as the first research object due to its exceptional stability. Using ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}{\mathsf{^{-}}}$ , ${\mathsf{C l O}}_{4}^{-}$ , SCN-, I- or $\mathsf{G d m^{+}}$ as a mediator and acetic acid (AA) as a modulator, UiO-66(Zr) MOFs adopted an hcp (hexagonal close packing) topology can be facilely obtained in aqueous- phase at $90^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figures 2a, 2b and Figures S4 to S6 in ESI†). hcp-UiO-66 is a ligand-deficient analogue to the conventional fcu-UiO-66, while the structure of its secondary building units is $Z r_{12}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathsf{O})_{8}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H})_{8}(\upmu_{2}{-}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H})_{6}$ which can be viewed as a fusion of two commonly observed $z_{\r_{\r_{6}}}$ clusters.20,38 Previous study has disclosed that the formation of hcp structure was due to the low solubility of ligands in the synthesis media and the presence of massive modulators.38 Therefore, the limited solubilization capacity of the applied SS in combination with the competition of AA rationalizes the generation of hcp phase structure by the developed SSISA strategy. ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms of hcp-UiO-66-NaI and hcp-UiO66-Gdm exhibit type I isotherms with BET surface area of 895 and $817~\mathsf{m}^{2}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure 2c). SEM and TEM images show an intergrowth morphology of flat plates with approximately $200\ \mathsf{n m}$ in diameter and $20\ n m$ in thickness (Figure 2d, 2e) in good agreement with those typically observed for hcp MOFs.20,38 TGA shows that the hcp-UiO-66-NaI is stable up to $450~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The weight loss of $39.6\%$ from 300 to $800~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ matches well with the theoretical weight loss of $40.5\%$ based on the conversion of $\mathsf{Z r}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{15}\left(\mathsf{B D C}\right)_{9}$ to $(Z r O_{2})_{12}$ (see ESI†, Figure S7).
When L-arginine was applied as a mediator, conventional fcuUiO-66 structure could be obtained with only eight equivalent L-arginine per $Z r^{4+}$ (Figure 2f). Given the more efficient solubilization of L-arginine, the formation of the fcu rather than hcp phase might be partly attributed to the increase of dissolved ligand concentration and the decrease of modulator amount. To verify the dual functions of L-arginine, GdmCl, glycine and lysine were chosen as additives for the control experiments. The results show that the addition of GdmCl without a carboxyl modulator moiety could only produce an amorphous sample. Meanwhile, no product could be obtained in the presence of glycine and lysine due to the lack of salting-in fragment. The sample synthesized via our SSISA strategy exhibits a BET surface area of $\boldsymbol{1286}\ \mathsf{m}^{2}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and similar thermal stability to conventional UiO-66 synthesized in organic media.19 In addition, $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectroscopic analysis shows that no L-arginine is incorporated into UiO-66 (see ESI†, Figure S8).
In addition to zirconium, UiO-66-type MOFs can also be built by other tetravalent metal ions such as ${\mathsf{H}}{\mathsf{f}}^{18}$ and Ce.22 UiO66(Hf) features similar physicochemical properties to UiO66(Zr) because Hf and Zr are of the same group elements. hcp and fcu topologies could be obtained using NaI and L-arginine as a mediator, respectively (Figure $\pmb{28}$ and Figure S9 in ESI†). Both UiO-66(Hf) MOFs exhibit a smaller specific surface area than UiO-66(Zr) counterparts due to the higher atomic mass of Hf (see ESI†, Figure S9). The Ce-based UiO-66 is another typical member of UiO-66-type MOFs. The initial reported synthesis for UiO-66(Ce) was to treat cerium(IV) ammonium nitrate $((\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{2}\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{6})$ and BDC in a mixture of DMF and water at 100 $^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.^{22}$ Here, using our SSISA strategy, UiO-66(Ce) can be achieved at room temperature with water as the sole solvent (Figure 2g and Figure S10 in ESI†). Notably, strong oxidative SS of ${\mathsf{N a C l O}}_{4}$ should be selected in current case to prohibit the reduction of Ce(IV) and to promote the aqueous-phase synthesis of MOFs. In contrast, the construction of MOFs cannot be realized upon using NaI as a mediator because of the redox reaction between $\mathsf{I}^{-}$ and ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ .
