Besides tetravalent metals, trivalent ones are also competent to construct stable MOFs, and typical examples are MIL series pioneered by Férey and co-workers.23,24 Of special interest is MIL-53(Al) which exhibits breathing effect in the presence of water molecules.23 Conventionally, MIL-53(Al) is hydrothermally constructed from $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ and BDC at 220 $^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for 3 days. Here, by simply adding NaI, we successfully prepared MIL-53(Al) at $100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with shortened time to $24\ h$ (Figure 3a). The XRD pattern of the as-synthesized MIL-53 $(A l)_{\mathsf{a s}}$ coincides well with that of MOFs containing unreacted BDC ligands in the tunnels (Figure 3b).39,40 Replacing I- with ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}{\mathrm{\cdot}}$ , ${\mathsf{C l O}}_{4}{\mathsf{\bar{\Psi}}}$ or $\mathsf{G d m^{+}}$ yields the same results (see ESI†, Figure S11a). After further solvent extraction, the XRD pattern evolves into the form of $N/(-53(A l)_{1\mathrm{t}}$ $(1\mathsf{t}=\mathsf{l o w}$ temperature) because of the reversible adsorption of water at room temperature (Figure 3b). Compared to $M1L-53(A l)_{\mathsf{a s},}$ the absence of -COOH absorption at $1700~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ in FTIR spectrum of $N\vert\mathsf{L}\bar{\mathsf{-}}53(\mathsf{A}\vert)_{\mathsf{s o l}}$ indicates the effective removal of free BDC in tunnels (Figure 3c). The calculated BET surface area is $1564{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}\ {\bf g}^{-1}.$ , being higher than that of the reported one.23,39 The TGA profile indicates that the sample is thermally stable up to $500~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in consistency with previous report (see ESI†, Figure S11).23,39
Different from the thermodynamically favored MOFs mentioned above, MIL-101(Cr) is a kinetic product and the synthesis conditions are subtle. The resulting framework adopts an augmented MTN zeotype topology with two kinds of quasispherical cages (Figure 3d).24 The significant attraction of MIL101(Cr) lies in its unusually large pore volume and surface area. Traditionally, MIL-101(Cr) is formed between 180 and $220^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ involving the use of highly corrosive HF as a mineralizing agent.41 Moreover, a significant amount of BDC remains unreacted and trapped in cages and is hard to remove, leading to the decrease of porosity. Nevertheless, our SSISA strategy could also efficiently work for the synthesis of MIL-101(Cr) (see $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{E S l}}^{+}$ , section S5.6.1). Assisted by NaI, highly crystallized MIL101(Cr) can be obtained without HF at relatively low
temperature of ${}^{120^{\circ}\mathsf C}.$ . SEM image shows that the size of particles is around $250\ \mathsf{n m}$ , and no rod-like by-products of recrystallized BDC are observable (Figure 3e). Their experimental XRD pattern is quite in accord with the simulated one (Figure 3f). The ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm exhibits the characteristic steps of the MIL-101 structure with a high specific BET surface area of $3314~\mathsf{m}^{2}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 3g). When using GdmCl as a mediator, the produced MOFs are of the similar structure to that prepared from NaI (see ESI†, Figure S15).
To further illustrate the extensibility of the strategy, various functionalized BDC ligands have also been applied to construct stable MOFs, and here we present some typical examples (Figure $\pmb{4}\mathsf{a})$ ). In the presence of SS, UiO-66(Zr) derivatives containing $-N H_{2},$ $-N O_{2},$ -OH, -COOH, $-C H_{3}$ and -Br could be facilely synthesized in aqueous-phase with excellent crystallinity (Figures 4b and Figures S16, S17 and Table S2 in ESI†). Notably, UiO-66(Ce) derivatives containing - ${\mathsf{-N O}}_{2},$ , - $C H_{3}$ and -Br can be obtained without any additive except SS at room temperature within 10 min. No change in the crystallinity of the product is observed upon scaling-up the synthesis to 100 fold (Figure 4c and Figures S18, S19 in $\pmb{\mathrm{\sf\sf\sf\sf}}\in\mathsf{S l}^{+}$ ). The simple, mild, rapid and aqueous approach makes it possible for mass production and commercialization. In the case of MIL-101(Cr)- $\cdot N H_{2},$ it is conventionally obtained by post-synthetic modificViaetwioArnticldeuOen itnoe the readily decomposition of BDC-NH2DOlIi:g1a0.n1d039a/tC9eSlCe0v1a4t4e7Kd temperature (Figure 4d). By SSISA strategy, the direct synthesis of MIL-101(Cr)- $\cdot N H_{2}$ was successfully realized at ${\mathsf{120}}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ with the aid of GdmCl as a mediator. Slightly broadened Bragg reflection is due to the small particle size of the resulting sample (Figure 4e and Figure S20a in  ESI†). The specific surface area of the sample has reached a satisfactory $2947\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (see  ESI†, Figure S20b). $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR shows that BDC- $\cdot N H_{2}$ ligand was intact without any decomposition (Figure 4f).42
## Conclusions
In summary, Hofmeister effect is firstly introduced to rationalize the crystallization of stable MOFs in aqueous-phase and thereafter a general SSISA strategy is proposed based on a unique “solubilization-mediating” mechanism. Tens of typical and useful stable MOFs are exemplified to validate its reproducibility. The SSISA strategy could not only simplify the traditional organic-phase self-assembly process of MOFs (e.g., low temperature, rapid reaction rate, no mineralizer and green solvent) but also help realize the formation of different phase structures (e.g., hcp-UiO-66 and fcu-UiO-66). This study, as we believe, would pave the way for the large-scale and economic production of MOFs. Further extension of this strategy could provide a new perspective for the development of a broad variety of new stable MOFs in aqueous phase.
